\title[The Refinement Hierarchy of Survival Games]{The Refinement Hierarchy of Survival
Games: Bounded-Depth Clocks, the $\omega^{\omega}$ Limit, and an Exact Closure Ordinal}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 11, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop the order-theoretic theory of \emph{survival games}: two-player games in which one
player (Mortal) fixes a well-ordered schedule of moments and survives exactly as long as that
schedule lasts, so that the strategic content of the game is the order type of the schedule.
We study the operator that \emph{refines} a schedule by replacing each of its moments with an
entire copy of the natural-number clock, and prove that refinement multiplies the survival
value on the left by $\omega$. Iterating, the $k$-fold refinement of a game of value $v$ has
value $\omega^{k}\cdot v$; in particular the $k$-fold refinement of the natural-number
schedule has value exactly $\omega^{k+1}$, and the hierarchy is strict in the strong sense
that a $j$-fold refined Mortal cannot force survival to the death round of a $k$-fold refined
one whenever $j<k$. We characterise the reach of bounded-depth clocks: a game is timed by the
$k$-fold lexicographic natural-number clock if and only if its survival value is at most
$\omega^{k}$, the general statement being that a game embeds into the canonical well-order of
an ordinal $o$ exactly when its value is at most $o$. We construct the limit of all finite
refinement depths as a dependent lexicographic sum and prove its value is exactly
$\omega^{\omega}$; the upper bound comes from an explicit key function
$\key\langle k,a\rangle=\omega^{k}+\rank(a)$, which simultaneously certifies well-foundedness
of the dependent lexicographic order. This is generalised to a limit theorem: a lexicographic
chain of lives whose values lie below and are cofinal in an additively principal ordinal $o$
has value exactly $o$. We prove completeness of the survival value as an invariant,
non-commutativity of concatenation of lives, and a criterion for refinement stability
($\omega^{a}$ is stable iff $\omega\le a$), and deduce a negative result refuting the natural
conjecture that refinement always strictly increases survival: the limit clock is a fixed
point, $\omega\cdot\omega^{\omega}=\omega^{\omega}$. Finally we exhibit a monotone transfinite
transition system whose stages admit a closed form, whose reachable-time well-order is
order-isomorphic to the moments of the bounded-nondeterministic game, and whose closure
ordinal is exactly $\omega^{2}$.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{ordinal arithmetic, well-orders, survival games, lexicographic orders, Cantor normal
